<Doc
  title="Monitors and alerts"
  description="How mcpd watches error rate, latency, cost, score means, structural divergence and run repetition, why a monitor latches, why a quiet window never clears a standing alert, and its webhook."
  lede="A condition worth watching, watched. mcpd evaluates on a timer, latches what it finds, and tells you once."
  og="docs"
>
  <h2>What a monitor is</h2>
  <p>
    A monitor is a saved question mcpd asks itself on a timer: is the error rate
    in this project above five percent over the last five minutes? Is the mean
    of this scorer below the number we agreed on? It reads the same storage the
    interface reads, so a monitor can only assert something you could have
    looked up yourself.
  </p>
  <p>
    Monitors are off until you configure one. mcpd does not guess a threshold
    for you, because a threshold that nobody chose is a threshold nobody trusts.
  </p>

  <MonitorChart class="not-prose my-8" />

  <h2>It latches</h2>
  <p>
    A monitor that fires on every evaluation while a condition persists gets
    muted within a day, and a muted monitor is worse than no monitor because it
    looks like coverage. So mcpd reports the transition and not the state: once
    on the way into trouble, once on the way out, and nothing in between.
  </p>

  <h2>Silence is not recovery</h2>
  <p>
    This is the rule that costs people outages. When traffic stops, the window
    holds too few runs to judge, and the obvious implementation reports
    &ldquo;nothing wrong&rdquo; and clears the alert. But an agent that has
    stopped serving requests is the failure, not the recovery from it.
  </p>
  <p>
    A window with too few runs in it leaves a standing alert exactly where it
    was. A monitor returns to nominal only when a reading with enough samples
    behind it comes back inside the threshold.
  </p>
  <p>
    The same reasoning sets <code>min_samples</code>. One failed run in a quiet
    hour is a hundred percent error rate, and mcpd will not call that a fault.
    The default is twenty.
  </p>

  <h2>What can be watched</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Watch</th><th>Measures</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><code>error_rate</code></td>
        <td>Failed runs over total runs in the window.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>latency_p95</code></td>
        <td>Ninety-fifth percentile run duration, in milliseconds.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>cost</code></td>
        <td>Total spend in the window, in the price book's currency.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>score_mean</code></td>
        <td>
          Mean of one scorer, named by <code>scorer_id</code>. This is the one
          that catches a quality regression a latency chart cannot see.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>trace_count</code></td>
        <td>Runs in the window. Watch it with <code>below</code> to catch a pipeline that went silent.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>step_count_divergence</code></td>
        <td>
          Runs in the window that took an unusual number of steps for their own
          definition. The only watch here with no threshold in the units of the
          thing it measures. It reads durable runs, not traces.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>run_repetition</code></td>
        <td>
          How much a run repeats itself: the mean, over the runs in the window,
          of the most attempts any one of them made at a single node of its
          definition. One means no node ran twice. It reads durable runs, not
          traces.
        </td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>When you cannot name the number</h2>
  <p>
    Every watch above compares a measurement against a ceiling you chose. That
    works while you know what the measurement should be. The hardest runaway to
    catch is the one that never reaches any ceiling you would have thought to
    set: a run that loops eight times instead of twice, finishes, costs four
    times what it should, and reports no error at all. Its step count is not
    high in absolute terms. It is high <em>for that definition</em>, and only
    that definition&rsquo;s own recent runs can say so.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>step_count_divergence</code> sets no threshold in steps. For each
    definition version it takes the step counts of that version&rsquo;s recently
    completed runs, takes the middle of them as the usual shape, and reports the
    runs that sit further out than a standardised bound allows. Nobody has to know
    that <code>checkout</code> normally takes eleven steps, and nobody has to
    revisit the number when it starts taking fourteen.
  </p>

  <h3>The statistic, and why it is this one</h3>
  <p>
    The measure of how far out a run sits is the modified z-score:
    <span class="data">0.6745 &times; (steps &minus; median) &divide; MAD</span>,
    where MAD is the median of the absolute deviations from the median. A run
    diverges when that score passes <code>z_bound</code>, which defaults to
    <span class="data">3.5</span>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Both constants are the conventional ones and neither is arbitrary. For
    normally distributed data the median absolute deviation converges to
    0.6745&sigma; rather than to &sigma;, so the raw ratio is measured in units
    of about two thirds of a standard deviation. Multiplying by 0.6745 puts it
    back into units of &sigma;, which is what lets 3.5 mean what everyone reads
    it to mean. 3.5 is the bound published with the statistic, about one run in
    two thousand on normal data. Step counts are not normally distributed, so
    treat it as the value to tune away from rather than a promised false alarm
    rate.
  </p>
  <p>
    The median and the MAD are used rather than a mean and a standard deviation
    because they survive the thing being looked for. A single run that took
    forty times the usual number of steps moves a mean and inflates a standard
    deviation enough to hide itself; the median absolute deviation tolerates up
    to half its sample being outliers before it moves at all.
  </p>

  <h3>Built-in safeguards</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>A sample large enough to judge.</strong> A definition version needs
      <span class="data">12</span> completed runs in the window before the watch
      will compare anything against it. The median of twelve sits between the
      sixth and seventh value, so no single run decides where the middle is.
      Below that, one unusual run moves the middle itself, so the baseline
      describes the outlier rather than the norm. A version with too few reads
      as insufficient, which leaves a standing alert exactly where it was. The
      twelve are counted inside the monitor&rsquo;s own window, so set the two
      together: a definition that runs a few times an hour needs a
      <code>window_seconds</code> measured in hours to reach them.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>No division by zero.</strong> A definition with no loop and no branch
      takes the same number of steps every time, so its MAD is zero and every
      departure from it is infinitely anomalous. Emitting infinity is not an
      answer: it cannot be compared, it prints as <code>inf</code> in a sentence
      somebody has to act on, and it reaches a webhook as <code>null</code>.
      mcpd falls back to the mean absolute deviation, scaled by
      &radic;(&pi;/2) so it estimates the same &sigma;. When even that is zero,
      because every run in the sample took exactly the same number of steps,
      there is no ratio to report and the alert says it in steps instead:
      &ldquo;every one of the last 12 completed runs took 9&rdquo;. In that one
      case <code>z_bound</code> does not apply, because there is no score to
      hold against it: raising the bound widens what counts as ordinary
      variation, and a cohort with no variation has none to widen.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>One version of a definition at a time.</strong> Runs are grouped by
      definition <em>version</em>. A new version is a deliberate change of
      shape, so a run of v3 is not anomalous for differing from a run of v2, and
      pooling them makes a two-humped sample whose median describes neither. A
      freshly published version therefore reports nothing until it has twelve
      completed runs of its own, which is the right way round.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>One report per failure.</strong> Only completed runs join the
      baseline, and only a completed run is reported for taking too
      <em>few</em> steps. Ending short is what failing, being cancelled, timing
      out and not having finished yet all mean, and this is the one watch that
      exists to see what the error rate cannot. A run still in flight is scored,
      though: a run that has already gone long is reported while it is still
      going, which is the whole point of the watch.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Configuring it</h3>
  <p>
    This watch has two numbers because it asks two questions.
    <code>z_bound</code> asks how far out a single run has to be, and it is a
    standardised score, so the same 3.5 serves a definition that usually takes
    four steps and one that usually takes four hundred. <code>threshold</code>
    then asks how many such runs a window may hold before anyone is told, which
    is an operational choice. Set it to <code>0</code> with
    <code>above</code> to be told about any of them. <code>below</code> has no
    use on this watch: every run being ordinary is the healthy state, not the
    one worth waking somebody for.
  </p>

  <pre><code class="language-json">{`{
  "monitors": [
    {
      "id": "checkout-shape",
      "name": "A checkout run took an unusual number of steps",
      "project": "support-desk",
      "watch": "step_count_divergence",
      "compare": "above",
      "threshold": 0,
      "z_bound": 3.5,
      "window_seconds": 3600,
      "webhook": "https://hooks.example.com/mcpd"
    }
  ]
}`}</code></pre>

  <p>
    The measured value is the number of runs that diverged, so
    <code>compare</code> and <code>threshold</code> count runs and not steps.
    The detail names the worst three: <code>checkout v1 run r_9f2 took 40 steps
    against a usual 10 (z 20.2)</code>.
  </p>
  <p>
    This watch reads the durable run store rather than stored traces, because
    only a run header knows which definition version a run pinned and how many
    steps it has taken. <code>run_repetition</code> below reads it too, and
    those two are the only ones that do. A daemon that serves no runs reports
    this as insufficient and says so. <code>min_samples</code> is not read here:
    the sample that matters is per definition version, not per window.
  </p>

  <h2>When a run keeps doing the same thing</h2>
  <p>
    Divergence asks how far a run strays from the shape its own definition
    usually takes, and it needs a cohort of recent runs before it can answer.
    There is a narrower question that needs no cohort at all: how many times did
    a run go round the same node?
  </p>
  <p>
    Two faults hide from the error rate here. A loop that keeps re-entering one
    node succeeds, and a retry storm that gets there in the end is a success.
    Both do the same work over and over while the error rate stays at zero. It
    is also the reading a step count cannot give on its own: a run of forty
    steps that visited forty nodes once is a long definition, and a run of forty
    steps that visited one node forty times is stuck.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>run_repetition</code> counts attempts. For each run in the window it
    takes the number of attempts made at each node of that run&rsquo;s
    definition, keeps the largest, and reads the mean of that number across the
    window. A retry writes another attempt at the node it retried, and a loop
    iteration writes a new position at the node it re-entered, so both count.
    <span class="data">1</span> means no node ran twice.
  </p>
  <p>
    Unlike divergence, this one has a threshold in the units it measures. A
    definition that is forty steps long and a definition that is four steps long
    both read <span class="data">1</span> when nothing repeats, so the number
    means the same thing in every project and you can name a ceiling for it.
    That makes it an ordinary threshold watch, and only its reading of the run
    store is borrowed from the watch above.
  </p>

  <h3>What it counts</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>A fan-out counts.</strong> A <code>foreach</code> over nine items
      is nine attempts at one node, and the journal cannot tell that apart from
      a loop that went round nine times: both are indexed positions under one
      node, and only the definition knows which construct wrote them. So a
      project whose definitions fan out sits high, and its threshold has to be
      set for that. Every evaluation carries its value, tripped or not, so read
      what an ordinary window reports before you choose one.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A skipped position does not count.</strong> A false
      <code>when</code> guard writes an attempt row and the node never ran, so
      counting it would let a <code>foreach</code> over a filtered list read as
      repetition.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A run that attempted nothing is left out.</strong> Not counted as
      zero: it has not repeated anything because it has not run anything, and a
      zero in the sample would pull the mean below the
      <span class="data">1</span> that the threshold is set against.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A run still in flight is counted.</strong> That is most of the
      point. A run stuck in a retry loop is in the reading while it is still
      looping, rather than after it finally gives up.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Configuring it</h3>
  <p>
    One number, unlike divergence. <code>threshold</code> is the mean attempt
    count the window may reach before anyone is told, and
    <span class="data">1.2</span> is a fifth of a window&rsquo;s runs going round
    twice. <code>below</code> has no use here: every run in the sample attempted
    at least one node at least once, so the reading has a floor of
    <span class="data">1</span> and can never fall through it.
  </p>

  <pre><code class="language-json">{`{
  "monitors": [
    {
      "id": "checkout-repetition",
      "name": "Checkout runs are going round the same step",
      "project": "support-desk",
      "watch": "run_repetition",
      "compare": "above",
      "threshold": 1.2,
      "window_seconds": 3600,
      "min_samples": 20,
      "webhook": "https://hooks.example.com/mcpd"
    }
  ]
}`}</code></pre>

  <p>
    <code>min_samples</code> is read here, and it counts the runs the watch read
    from the run store rather than traces, so a deployment whose spans are
    ingested somewhere else is still judged on its runs. A window under it
    reports as insufficient, which leaves a standing alert exactly where it was.
  </p>
  <p>
    The detail names the worst three runs and the node each one went round:
    <code>checkout v1 run r_9f2 attempted settle 11 times</code>. Runs that
    repeated nothing are not named, because there is nothing in them to open.
  </p>

  <h2>An objective, when a threshold is the wrong question</h2>
  <p>
    A monitor asks whether the error rate is above five percent right now. An
    SLO asks something more useful: are we going to miss the target we promised,
    and how fast are we heading there.
  </p>
  <p>
    The difference matters because a threshold cannot tell a two minute blip
    apart from a slow leak, and those want opposite responses. Set a threshold
    tight enough to catch the leak and the blip pages you at three in the
    morning. Set it loose enough to sleep through the blip and the leak runs for
    a week.
  </p>

  <h3>The budget, and the rate it burns</h3>
  <p>
    An objective of <code>0.995</code> over thirty days says half a percent of
    runs may fail. That half percent is the error budget. A burn rate of 1 spends
    it exactly over the thirty days; a burn rate of 10 spends it in three.
  </p>
  <p>
    mcpd measures the burn over two windows at once and trips only when both
    agree: faster than <span class="data">14.4x</span> over the last hour
    <em>and</em> faster than <span class="data">6x</span> over the last six. That
    is what separates the two cases. A five minute outage moves the one hour
    number and cannot move the six, so it does not page. A leak that has been
    running all afternoon moves both.
  </p>
  <p>
    Worth knowing before you rely on it: on a 99.5% objective those thresholds
    are an error rate of about 7.2%. A steady 5% failure rate burns the budget
    ten times too fast and will exhaust it early, but it does not wake anyone.
    Watch <code>budget_remaining</code> for that, and keep a
    <code>score_mean</code> or <code>error_rate</code> monitor if you want to
    know sooner.
  </p>

  <pre><code class="language-json">{`{
  "slos": [
    {
      "id": "support-availability",
      "name": "99.5% of support runs succeed over 30 days",
      "project": "support-desk",
      "objective": 0.995,
      "window_days": 30,
      "min_samples": 20,
      "webhook": "https://hooks.example.com/mcpd"
    }
  ]
}`}</code></pre>

  <p>
    <code>GET /v1/api/slos</code> returns each objective with its success rate,
    the fraction of budget left, and both burn rates.
    <code>POST /v1/api/slos/&#123;id&#125;/ack</code> acknowledges one. They
    latch and acknowledge exactly as monitors do, and a window too thin to judge
    never clears a standing alert.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>budget_remaining</code> goes negative once the budget is overspent,
    rather than stopping at zero. Zero and "missed the target by a factor of a
    hundred" are different situations and the number says which.
  </p>

  <h2>Configuring one</h2>
  <p>
    Monitors live in a JSON file rather than on the command line, because a
    threshold, a window and a comparison do not fit on a flag and should be
    reviewed like code. The file mounts as a Kubernetes ConfigMap unchanged.
  </p>

  <pre><code class="language-json">{`{
  "monitor_interval_seconds": 60,
  "monitors": [
    {
      "id": "support-error-rate",
      "name": "Support agent error rate above 5%",
      "project": "support-desk",
      "watch": "error_rate",
      "compare": "above",
      "threshold": 0.05,
      "window_seconds": 300,
      "min_samples": 20,
      "webhook": "https://hooks.example.com/mcpd"
    },
    {
      "id": "relevance-regression",
      "name": "Answer relevance fell below 0.8",
      "project": "support-desk",
      "watch": "score_mean",
      "scorer_id": "answer_relevance",
      "compare": "below",
      "threshold": 0.8,
      "window_seconds": 3600
    }
  ]
}`}</code></pre>

  <Command
    class="not-prose my-6"
    title="start with monitors armed"
    command="mcpd serve --config-file ./mcpd.json"
  />

  <p>
    An unknown key fails the start rather than being ignored. A typo in
    <code>threshold</code> that silently watches nothing is the worst outcome
    available, so mcpd refuses it out loud.
  </p>
  <p>
    Set <code>"enabled": false</code> to keep a monitor's definition without
    evaluating it. <code>mcpd config</code> prints how many are armed.
  </p>

  <h2>Where a transition goes</h2>
  <p>
    Every transition is logged with the monitor id, the project, the threshold
    and the measured value as structured fields, so a log pipeline can route it
    without parsing a sentence. If you already run one, that is the whole
    integration.
  </p>
  <p>
    Give a monitor a <code>webhook</code> and mcpd also POSTs the transition.
    The body is generic on purpose: Slack, PagerDuty, Opsgenie and every
    workflow tool accept an inbound webhook, and shaping the payload for one of
    them would mean maintaining a set of vendor integrations that go stale.
    With several replicas on one store, one of them at a time holds the
    monitors lease and posts the transition, so a receiver gets it once.
  </p>

  <pre><code class="language-json">{`{
  "monitor_id": "support-error-rate",
  "name": "Support agent error rate above 5%",
  "project": "support-desk",
  "condition": "tripped",
  "watch": "error_rate",
  "threshold": 0.05,
  "value": 0.5,
  "samples": 40,
  "detail": "error rate is 0.5000, threshold above 0.0500, over 40 run(s)",
  "at": "2026-08-28T02:13:54.687391446Z"
}`}</code></pre>

  <p>
    A failed delivery is logged and dropped. Retrying would either block the
    evaluation loop or need a durable outbound queue, and an alerting path that
    can quietly fall behind is worse than one that visibly missed a message.
  </p>

  <h2>Reading and acknowledging</h2>
  <p>
    <code>GET /v1/api/monitors</code> returns every monitor with its latched
    condition, its last reading and when it tripped.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>POST /v1/api/monitors/&#123;id&#125;/ack</code> acknowledges one.
    Acknowledging silences the transition without pretending the condition
    cleared: the monitor stays acknowledged while it still breaches, and returns
    to nominal on its own when the number recovers. Nobody has to remember to
    un-mute it.
  </p>
  <p>
    Monitor state is held in memory and rebuilt from readings on every start. It
    is not persisted, because a restart that resurrects an alert which has
    already recovered is a worse failure than a restart that re-evaluates.
  </p>

